# Windows 10 capture

The guest is the powered-off VMware disk `D:\VMware\Windows 10 x64\Windows 10 x64.vmdk`. QEMU opens it with `format=vmdk` and `-snapshot`. `vmrun list` must show no running VMs first. Do not write the VMDK. Boot with TCG, `-m 8192`, and `-smp 4`. Do not pass `-accel whpx` to `qemu-system-x86_64`. The VerOS NG boot uses the same memory and CPU count.

Two captures exist. Both stay under `output/`, which is gitignored.

- `tools/windows-trace` records numeric syscall lines: number, status, and `a1`–`a4`. No names and no ordinal map.
- `tools/syscall-diff` runs one GPL amd64 `probe.exe` on Windows 10 22H2 x64 and on the amd64 ReactOS boot. It calls the names in `compat/reference/win10-22h2-syscalls.txt` through `GetProcAddress` and prints `DIFF <name> <status>`, `MISSING`, `CRASH`, or `STARTED`, then `DIFF END OK`. COM1 is `-serial file:` with forward slashes. A FAT sink copy is only a backup.

A Windows result is evidence only when that guest log contains `DIFF END OK`. A host run of the probe is not a Windows 10 22H2 result.

`tools/syscall-diff`, `tools/graphics-diff`, and `tools/kernel-abi` reuse `output/<tool>/windows.log` when `tools/probe-stamp/Test-WindowsProbe.ps1` prints `REUSE` for that tool. The stamp is `output/<tool>/windows.probe`. It records the probe source hash and the log hash. A change to that probe's sources requires a new Windows 10 boot. Each runner's `-CheckWindows` reports the same decision.

The tree may keep the probe source, the name list, the compare script, and a report of names plus status codes. It may not keep the VMDK, an ISO, a disk image, a trace, a disassembly, a header, a `.lib`, a `.sys`, or an object file.

```text
Right: DIFF NtCreateFile 0x00000000
Wrong: a function body, a Windows ordinal, or a pasted WDK declaration
```
